[Music]  he's a young lay is thirty and from  Somalia she doesn't want to show her  face but she's committed to telling how  she was driven from her home my husband  was killed and I was at home with my  children  masked men came into our house they  raped me and beat me I still have the  scars from it that's why I came here is  one of around 1,500 refugees evacuated  by the United Nations from Libyan camps  last autumn they have endured war and  many of them sexual violence every one  of these women was deemed especially  vulnerable by UN staff who arranged for  them to be flown out there now  recovering in this shelter for women and  children in each air before being flown  to Europe funding for the shelter comes  from the EU it's a prime example of  legal migration without the pitfalls of  a perilous Mediterranean crossing  refugees arrive here from detention in  Libya we provide them with immediate  assistance with protection we have a  team of psychologists working with them  so we have what we call a philosophy of  care basically we provide them with  recreational activities with counseling  with we provide them with language  classes to prepare them for resettlement  neasha is a former French colony and one  of the poorest countries in the world it  also has the world's highest birth rate  on average women in Neesha have six  children there's little infrastructure  and economic output is low the West  African nation is Africa's main transit  hub for Refugees and people smuggling is  big business the President of the  European Parliament has come to assess  the situation firsthand Antonio Tajani  says the EU aims to reverse the trend by  providing shelters so refugees are not  driven into the hands of smugglers we  have to stop these groups to profit from  the lives of Africans who died in the  desert and in the seas we have to stop  them completely absolutely mother in  order to help need air support the  refugees as they wait for a resettlement  Europe the EU is pumping money into the  local economy most people like those  here in the capital in the army live  hand-to-mouth  this woman sells snacks for about one  cent each but not many people buy these  men sell tea on the street sometimes  it's enough to buy a phone card  despite rampant poverty measures  president says his country is ready to  help but long term it will need much  more money from the EU it's important  that we wage a tireless fight against  poverty through public but also through  private investment some of the refugees  in the shelter here in the capital have  been waiting more than a year to get to  Europe the UN has been sharply critical  of some EU countries that want nothing  to do with legal migration and refused a  refugee quota move more quickly which  would mean that we could save more lives  we could bring more people from  detention Tunisia he's is one of the few  who have made it this far  and she at least will have an  opportunity to start a new peaceful life  in Europe without having to put her fate  in the hands of smugglers

